{"name":"Aspen Snow","hex":"#f0f0e7","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"aspen","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Pertaining to the aspen tree.","Tremulous, trembling."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A poplar tree, especially of section Populus sect. Populus, of medium-size trees with thin, straight trunks of a greenish-white color.","The wood of such a tree; usually pale, lightweight and soft."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/aspen"},{"term":"snow","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The partly frozen, crystalline state of water that falls from the atmosphere as precipitation in flakes; also, the falling of such flakes; and the accumulation of them on the ground or on objects as a white layer.\n\n An instance of the falling of snow (etymology 1, noun sense 1); a snowfall; also, a snowstorm.","An instance of the falling of snow (etymology 1, noun sense 1); a snowfall; also, a snowstorm."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["Preceded by the dummy subject it: to have snow (noun etymology 1, noun sense 1) fall from the atmosphere.","Of a thing: to fall like snow."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/snow"}],"entries":[{"title":"Aspen","blocks":[{"text":"Aspen is a common name for certain tree species in the Populus sect. Populus, of the Populus (poplar) genus.\nAspens were referred to as \"asps\" in earlier forms of English."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Snowbowlaspens.jpg/330px-Snowbowlaspens.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aspen","wikidata":"Q1087592"},{"title":"Snow","blocks":[{"text":"Snow is a form of solid atmospheric precipitation consisting of individual ice crystals that grow while suspended in the air—usually within clouds—and then fall, accumulating on the ground, where they undergo further changes. It consists of frozen crystalline water throughout its life cycle, starting when, under suitable conditions, the ice crystals form in the atmosphere, increase to millimeter size, precipitate and accumulate on surfaces, then metamorphose in place, and ultimately melt, slide, or sublimate away."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/CargoNet_Di_12_Euro_4000_L%C3%B8nsdal_-_Bolna.jpg/330px-CargoNet_Di_12_Euro_4000_L%C3%B8nsdal_-_Bolna.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Snow","wikidata":"Q7561"},{"title":"Shades of white","blocks":[{"text":"Shades of white are colors that differ slightly from the CIE standard illuminant D65, a white point that represents the average color of daylight at noon. There isn't one objectively pure white, as noon daylight varies by location and atmospheric conditions, and the choice of using D65 instead of direct sunlight or a white point on the Planckian locus is arbitrary. For simplicity, this article will use the term pure white for the D65 white point."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Color_icon_white.svg/330px-Color_icon_white.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_white","wikidata":"Q7460350"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Aspen_trees_along_the_high_dirt_road_leading_to_Crested_Butte%2C_Colorado%2C_from_the_distant_Crystal_River_Valley_LCCN2015633794.tif/lossy-page1-500px-Aspen_trees_along_the_high_dirt_road_leading_to_Crested_Butte%2C_Colorado%2C_from_the_distant_Crystal_River_Valley_LCCN2015633794.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Aspen_trees_along_the_high_dirt_road_leading_to_Crested_Butte%2C_Colorado%2C_from_the_distant_Crystal_River_Valley_LCCN2015633794.tif/lossy-page1-1280px-Aspen_trees_along_the_high_dirt_road_leading_to_Cr